    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A <strong>manual under desk elliptical</strong> is the simplest version of the idea: two pedals, a flywheel, a resistance knob and your legs. Nothing moves unless you move it. That sounds like a limitation, but for most desk workers it is the reason to buy one.</p>
<p>Because you create every stride, a manual machine gives you more exercise per minute than a motorized one in passive mode. It also has no cord to trip over, no motor hum on your calls, and fewer parts that can fail. The trade-off is that you have to do the work, which is not ideal if mobility or strength is limited.</p>
<p>Below you will find what makes a good manual machine, how it compares with electric models, the five best manual picks based on specs and owner reviews, and tips to keep one from sliding or squeaking.</p>

<h2>How a Manual Under Desk Elliptical Works</h2>
<p>Each pedal is linked to a crank and a small flywheel inside the housing. When you push, the flywheel spins and stores momentum, which is what makes the stride feel smooth rather than jerky. A resistance system slows the flywheel down, and a knob lets you choose how strongly.</p>
<p>On the better machines that resistance is <strong>magnetic</strong>. Magnets sit close to the metal flywheel without touching it, and turning the knob moves them closer or farther away. Since nothing rubs, magnetic resistance stays quiet and consistent for years. Cheaper units sometimes use a felt pad or strap that presses on the wheel, which is louder and wears over time. Our guide to the <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-with-resistance/">under desk elliptical with resistance</a> explains each system and how many levels you actually need.</p>

<h2>Advantages of a Manual Under Desk Elliptical</h2>
<h3>A higher calorie burn per minute</h3>
<p>On a manual machine, effort is the default. Even at a light setting your quads, glutes and calves are working, which puts you around 2.0 METs for light seated pedaling and closer to 2.8 METs at a moderate pace, compared with about 1.3 for quiet sitting. For a 170 lb person that is roughly 160 to 225 calories an hour, depending on effort. A motorized machine in passive mode stays much closer to sitting. The motor-free design is the main reason manual machines win on calories.</p>
<h3>No power cord</h3>
<p>You can put a manual elliptical under any desk, sofa or kitchen table without hunting for an outlet. In an open-plan office, that also means no extension cable across a walkway.</p>
<h3>Quieter than any motor</h3>
<p>A good magnetic machine makes almost no sound beyond a soft whir. That is why manual models dominate our <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">quietest under desk elliptical</a> list. Electric models can be quiet too, but a motor always adds some hum.</p>
<h3>Fewer things to break</h3>
<p>No motor, controller board or remote means fewer failure points. Most manual problems come down to a squeak or a loose pedal bolt, which you can fix yourself.</p>
<h3>You set the pace</h3>
<p>You can slow down while you write and speed up while you listen. Many people find a self-paced rhythm easier to sustain for long periods than a fixed motor speed.</p>

<h2>Manual vs Electric Under Desk Elliptical</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Manual</th><th>Electric / automatic</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who drives the pedals</td><td>You</td><td>A motor (you can pedal along)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calorie burn</td><td>Higher by default</td><td>Low in passive mode, higher if you push</td></tr>
<tr><td>Noise</td><td>Very quiet with magnetic resistance</td><td>Low motor hum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power</td><td>None (small battery for the display)</td><td>Needs an outlet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Most desk workers who can pedal comfortably</td><td>Limited mobility, circulation goals, low stamina</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>If pedaling under your own power is difficult, a motor makes sense. Our <a href="/automatic-under-desk-elliptical/">automatic under desk elliptical</a> guide explains passive and active modes, and the <a href="/electric-elliptical-under-desk/">electric elliptical under desk</a> guide covers cords, remotes and motor noise.</p>

<h2>The 5 Best Manual Under Desk Ellipticals</h2>
<p>These picks are ranked on smoothness, noise, resistance range and brand support, based on specs and owner reviews. All use adjustable resistance and are designed for seated use only.</p>
[[product:cubii-jr2|1]]
<p>The Cubii JR2 is the benchmark. Its magnetic flywheel is near-silent, the motion is smooth and knee-friendly, and Cubii sells replacement parts. If you want Bluetooth tracking, the [[amazon:cubii-jr2-plus|Cubii JR2+]] adds app sync.</p>
[[product:deskcycle-ellipse|2]]
<p>The DeskCycle Ellipse has a low pedal height, which makes it the best manual choice for standard-height desks and tall users. Its resistance range goes from gentle to genuinely tiring. It takes up more floor space than the mini machines, so check the area under your desk as well as the height.</p>
[[product:sunny-sf-e3872|3]]
<p>Sunny Health &amp; Fitness gives you adjustable resistance and a smooth stride for a lot less than the premium brands. The display is basic, but the core experience is solid. It is the one to pick if you want to try a seated elliptical without spending premium money, or if you need a second machine for the office.</p>
[[product:lifepro-flexstride|4]]
<p>The Lifepro FlexStride is the manual pick for people who want their seated workout to feel like work. It has a wide resistance range and a stable base, though it is heavier to move. That weight helps it stay put when you push hard, which is exactly what you want at higher settings.</p>
[[product:geoneo|5]]
<p>Geoneo makes compact manual machines popular with home office workers. The lineup changes often, so confirm the listing mentions magnetic resistance and check recent reviews. You can [[search:Geoneo manual under desk elliptical|browse current Geoneo manual models]].</p>
<h3>Manual picks compared</h3>
